This condition is well-replicated. The mean across clean runs is roughly modulus ~220–250 bar, pore fraction ~0.65–0.77. This is a confirmed strong Pareto candidate. **Validation is established; resume searching.**

### Search Direction

The data shows a clear pattern: **lower polymer_wt (13–15 wt%) + cold bath (5–10°C) + moderate wait time (150–300s) + N2** tends to give high pore fraction. Higher modulus tends to come from higher polymer concentration or specific conditions.

The **14p8-0add-17deg-N2-1425s** condition (run2: 217/0.82) is intriguing for high pore fraction but has wrinkles and only 3 confirmed runs with high variability. The run4 (259.7/0.44) shows the pore fraction is highly variable — this condition is uncertain.

**Under-explored region:** polymer_wt ~14–15 with bath_temp ~6–8°C, moderate mixing_temp ~35–45°C, moderate wait ~150–250s, moderate pullcast speed ~8–12 mm/s. The recent campaign has heavily sampled pullcast_speed=5–7 mm/s. Slightly higher pullcast speed in the 8–12 range with this polymer/bath combination is under-sampled and could improve modulus while maintaining high pore fraction (faster casting → thinner, potentially more uniform skin → different phase separation kinetics).

**Proposed new point:** polymer_wt=14.5, bath_temp=7, mixing_temp=40, pullcast_speed=10, N2=true, wait=200s, nips=1600s. This is distinct from existing points (nearest is 14.5/bath=8/speed=7 and 14.5/bath=10/speed=7), explores slightly colder bath than 8°C and faster casting than 7 mm/s, which could push pore fraction higher while maintaining reasonable modulus.

```json
