  "mixing_temp": 40,
  "bath_temp": 7,
  "pullcast_speed": 10,
  "nitrogen": true,
  "coupon_to_bath_wait_time": 200,
  "nips_bath_wait_time": 1600,
  "polymer_wt": 14.5,
  "additive_wt": 0
}
```

**Expected benefit:** The colder bath (7°C vs. 8–10°C used recently) should promote faster phase separation and potentially higher pore fraction, consistent with the trend seen at bath_temp=8–9°C giving pore fractions of 0.63–0.77. The faster pullcast speed (10 vs. 5–7 mm/s) is under-sampled in this polymer_wt/bath_temp region and may influence film thickness and skin formation differently, potentially improving modulus without collapsing pore structure. This experiment fills a genuine gap in the (bath_temp=7, pullcast_speed=10, polymer_wt=14.5) subspace and will clarify whether the high-pore-fraction behavior observed at 14–15 wt% / cold bath extends to this slightly different casting speed and bath temperature combination.

